        "#interview"
      ],
      "text": "**The depth call**\n`#interview`\n`charts/chat-path-restructure/TODO.md:L166`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 9870
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/chat-path-restructure/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "8d511a4ae822e05f",
      "line_num": 174,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#interview"
      ],
      "text": "**Unfreeze the ledger**\n`#interview`\n`charts/chat-path-restructure/TODO.md:L174`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 9960
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-security/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "7363764882b12351",
      "line_num": 43,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What the buyer actually receives #interview #critical: the keystone card, and every other card sorts under it. An IT reviewer at a real-estate brokerage or a school board asks \"what is in this thing\". What lands in their hands \u2014 a signed CycloneDX file per sprig, an aggregate attestation for the whole instance, a page in the admin panel they can screenshot, a PDF we generate? The format decides whether the SBOM is a build artifact or a product surface, and those are different amounts of work. Name the reader before naming the mechanism.**\n`charts/sprig-security/TODO.md:L43`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10050
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-security/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "3282f73e2bb5a9d9",
      "line_num": 45,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What a community signature means #interview #critical: the S2 lane signs with the publisher's own key via the existing per-entry `pubkey`. That proves who built it and nothing about whether it is safe. Does Sage.is counter-sign a review, making us the vouching party and the liable one? Or is the publisher signature identity-only, with CVE policy enforced separately at graft time regardless of who signed? This is the hardest question on the chart and the buyer story rests on the answer. A reviewer who learns that \"signed\" means \"some stranger signed it\" will read every other claim differently.**\n`charts/sprig-security/TODO.md:L45`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10140
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-security/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "98e26c5913ddd702",
      "line_num": 48,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**CVE policy: what fails, and what gets waived #interview: CRITICAL only, or CRITICAL and HIGH? With or without a waiver path for findings unreachable from the sprig's actual entry points? The tension is concrete rather than theoretical \u2014 `unstructured` and `nltk` both carry criticals today, so a HIGH-fails policy with no waiver means the extractor sprigs that would absorb 76 alerts cannot publish at all. Decide the ceiling and the escape hatch together, because a policy with no waiver path gets bypassed instead of met.**\n`charts/sprig-security/TODO.md:L48`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10230
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-security/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "2ad2f6204a7455c1",
      "line_num": 50,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Where the re-scan runs, given no CI #interview #critical: `binary_sha256` pinning makes a sprig immutable, which is what makes the integrity story work and what makes patching impossible. A sprig published clean in June carries August's CVE, and no manifest exists for anything to read. This is precisely the job Dependabot does for `pyproject.toml` today and would stop doing after the strip. GitHub-hosted workflows are out of scope by decision, so name the home: a `make` target a person runs on a schedule, a job on the cluster, an advisory index published beside the artifacts that the catalog consults, or something else. A policy nobody re-runs decays to a snapshot of the day it was written.**\n`charts/sprig-security/TODO.md:L50`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10320
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-security/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "bb97a23be824ec27",
      "line_num": 53,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**SBOM format and where it rides #prototype: CycloneDX or SPDX, emitted by syft over each sprig's staging dir in `scripts/build-sprig-*.sh`, shipped as a third file in the OCI artifact beside `<tar>` and `<tar>.minisig`, signed with the same key. The zero-egress rule means it has to be readable offline with nothing fetched, which rules out any format that resolves references over the network. Prove the shape on one sprig \u2014 `sprig-rag-loaders` is the honest test because it is the one carrying the alerts \u2014 before touching the other thirteen build scripts.**\n`charts/sprig-security/TODO.md:L53`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10410
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-security/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "d6b3af743ee3950e",
      "line_num": 56,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Does `SPRIG_REQUIRE_SIGNED=1` already do half of this? #research: it exists as the per-instance signed-only ratchet. Read what it actually gates \u2014 graft only, or also the self-load path \u2014 and whether an operator can turn it on today and get a defensible answer. If it holds, the buyer story starts with a switch that already ships rather than with new code. Cheap card, resolve it early, it may shrink two others.**\n`charts/sprig-security/TODO.md:L56`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10500
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-security/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "aafc2f105d199840",
      "line_num": 58,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Do the policy fields ride M1 or follow it? #research: `sbom_sha256`, `scanned_at`, and `max_severity` have to live somewhere, and M1 already reopens the catalog schema to extract [supervisor.py:235](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/sprigs/supervisor.py#L235) into validated data. Reopening it twice is waste, but coupling this chart to M1's slice may stall both. Read the M1 design in [TODO.md:223](../../TODO.md#L223) and decide whether the fields go in that change or in a second one behind it.**\n`charts/sprig-security/TODO.md:L58`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10590
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-security/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "d8060762ec2122e5",
      "line_num": 60,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Licence obligations, not just CVEs #research: the Sprig Spec draft leads with licence compatibility, AI-UI ships AGPL-3.0, and a buyer's reviewer asks about licences at least as often as vulnerabilities. Does the SBOM carry licence data, does anything gate on it, and does a community publisher shipping an incompatibly-licensed dependency get caught before or after it reaches an instance? Establish whether this is one artifact answering two questions or two separate pieces of work.**\n`charts/sprig-security/TODO.md:L60`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10680
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-security/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "1dcfd9be56db9a17",
      "line_num": 62,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**A security section for Sprig Spec v1 #task: the draft has none. Whatever this chart decides about signatures, CVE ceilings, and SBOM carriage belongs in the spec before v1 freezes, or every conformant implementation inherits the hole. Write it once the decisions above exist, not before \u2014 a spec section written ahead of the decisions becomes the decision by default.**\n`charts/sprig-security/TODO.md:L62`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10770
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "c0cdcda271d17afb",
      "line_num": 59,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What a maker earns from a school deal #interview #critical: the keystone commercial card, and the only number Will actually asked for. Lane 2 is a resale margin, not a store fee \u2014 we carry the sales motion, the purchasing process, the IT and security review, the invoice, and first-line support, and the app rides inside a seat contract we already won. Opening position on the table is 50/50 of the attributable uplift, shifting toward the maker on renewal, because a renewal costs us a fraction of a new logo and the shift is arithmetic rather than a favour. Rung 3 should pay more than rung 0, so sovereign delivery is what makes the harder engineering worth a weekend. Open: the actual percentage, whether the shift is automatic or negotiated, and what \"attributable\" means on a bundled invoice. Do not settle it from a spreadsheet \u2014 settle it against one real school package. Note that the often-cited 30\u201350% channel margin range is unsourced and must be checked before it anchors anything.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L59`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10860
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "d62b693769ad0570",
      "line_num": 61,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**The Functions hole, which a listing policy does not close #critical: an earlier draft claimed that excluding Functions from the community lane closed the worst hole. That was wrong. Functions are a separate system from the sprig catalog, `POST /functions/load/url` accepts any GitHub URL today, and the `execute` event runs arbitrary JavaScript in the top frame. A listing policy governs what we advertise, not what is possible, and a push that brings thousands of self-installers makes that surface more exposed rather than less. Pick one and write it down: restrict or gate the endpoint, sandbox or remove the event, or accept the risk explicitly with the reasoning on file. The listing policy \u2014 community lane carries artifacts and tool-server listings only \u2014 is still correct and still cheap. It is just not the control.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L61`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 10950
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "ea99545f7ba0d4a0",
      "line_num": 63,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**The app pane, which is what the store is actually for #bonsai: the defining capability is not \"the assistant can call your API\". It is an installed app that opens in a pane the way Artifacts open, runs, and reports progress back. `Artifacts.svelte` already supplies the shell \u2014 side panel, resizable iframe, fullscreen, content switching. Four things stand between that and a store app. (a) The bridge validates by `event.origin`, which forces a false choice between a sandboxed app that cannot talk back and `allow-same-origin` on a `srcdoc`, which is effectively no sandbox at all; validating by `event.source === iframeElement.contentWindow` fixes it and everything else depends on that one change. (b) Service workers, localStorage, IndexedDB, and the Cache API all require a real origin, so a genuine PWA cannot run in the `srcdoc` pane and needs a same-origin URL \u2014 which only an in-container sprig provides, making rung 3 the only rung where \"PWA-type app\" is literally true. (c) Student progress must live where the school controls it, not in browser storage, so a per-user per-sprig state API is needed; if every maker invents their own, no school can audit any of it. (d) The spec reserves `ui-` and `agent-` but not `app-`. Not this quarter, but reserve the prefix now \u2014 it costs an hour and gets expensive once a third party binds to a shape we did not choose.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L63`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11040
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "8e24f3b19343a2cc",
      "line_num": 65,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Where a third-party app renders #interview: three candidate surfaces, none ready. `/home` (`app/src/routes/(app)/home/+page.svelte`) is Svelte with three placeholder cards from a hardcoded `soonWidgets` const, no slot and no persisted layout \u2014 cheapest to change, useless for a marketplace, because a card that needs a rebuild cannot be grafted. Spaces (`models/spaces.py`, ORM class still `Channel`, `ENABLE_SPACES` defaults False at `config.py:1389`) holds messages, threads, reactions, members, and agents, with no theming, files, tabs, or app slot; `data` is schemaless JSON and `data.agents[]` already lives there, so a `data.apps[]` needs no migration, but the pane layout and render component are all new. Chat has the most machinery, including a YouTube branch in `HTMLToken.svelte` that renders a trusted external origin at full privilege with no sandbox at all. Spike before deciding: stand up a throwaway same-origin HTML app, render it through the `<file type=\"html\">` path, and prove a postMessage round trip drives the chat input.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L65`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11130
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "694f9ec1b655ca5d",
      "line_num": 67,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Publisher agreement contents #interview: nothing exists. Needs IP ownership and licence grant, warranties, indemnity, data processing terms (see the student-data card), termination, and \u2014 the one nobody drafts until it bites \u2014 what happens to a school mid-contract when a publisher disappears, stops maintaining, or is acquired. A school that bought a bundle does not care whose fault it is. Decide whether we hold an escrow or a fallback right to keep serving a grafted artifact after a publisher exits.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L67`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11220
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "ba22119355d03907",
      "line_num": 69,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Student data, before any student touches it #critical: a tool server receives student input, and a hosted app receives more. FERPA, COPPA, and Canadian PIPEDA and provincial rules all bear. Needs a written data-handling position per partner, not a clause in a general agreement. Also decide the age-appropriate-content position, because we put a third party in front of minors under our brand, and the uptime position, because when the app is down the assistant fails a student and someone has to be on call.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L69`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11310
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "71e0be0558a3b864",
      "line_num": 71,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Egress disclosure as a product surface #interview: rung 0 and rung 2 both break the zero-egress promise on any instance that enables them, and zero egress is a load-bearing Sage.is claim. The operator must see the outbound call at the moment they enable it, not in a doc. Open: whether that is a badge in the store listing, a confirmation dialog at graft time, a line in the diagnostics panel, or all three. Check what the admin UI shows today when a tool server is added \u2014 if it shows nothing, this becomes a pre-push item rather than a design question.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L71`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11400
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "8e9dcde477aba9aa",
      "line_num": 73,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Attribution, when instances cannot phone home #interview: lane 2 pays a maker a share of what their app adds to a deal, which presumes we can tell that it did. Self-hosted instances do not report usage and should not be made to. So attribution is probably contractual rather than measured \u2014 a named line item on the invoice \u2014 and the card is really asking whether we ever need per-seat telemetry, and if so how an operator consents to it without breaking the sovereignty story.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L73`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11490
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "5378c7c2788c9422",
      "line_num": 75,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Support triage #interview: a school calls one vendor, by design, because that is the only way a bundle clears purchasing. So we take first-line support for a third party's software. Define the escalation path, the response expectation we can honestly offer, and what we owe a school when the maker is unreachable. This is a cost of lane 2 that the margin has to cover, and it belongs in the split conversation rather than beside it.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L75`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11580
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "7f037128c7cb90fc",
      "line_num": 77,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**When the submission lane opens, and what our signature means then #bonsai: deferred out of this quarter with the public lane, not resolved. Cross-links the hardest card on `charts/sprig-security/TODO.md`. Also decide the intake at that point: a publisher key and stable identity, a conforming `sprig.yaml`, and a pull request to a public index repo is the Etsy-simple shape, but it only works while volume is low. Name the volume at which it stops working.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L77`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11670
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "4a8def7c8d552dcb",
      "line_num": 79,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Payout mechanics, tax, and jurisdiction: deferred, named so it does not surprise us. Who pays whom, on what schedule, across which borders, with what withholding, and what happens when a maker is in a country our processor will not serve. None of this is urgent while lane 2 is a handful of negotiated deals, and all of it is urgent the week paid tiers open.**\n`charts/sprig-creator-program/TODO.md:L79`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11760
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "04ea66cc06c9fd7d",
      "line_num": 56,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**The thinking block eats the answer #critical: reported by Alexander 2026-08-03, reproduced same day. Symptom: `<thinking>` does not always close. Consequence: the model's answer is swallowed into the open block. On screen the assistant thinks, then says nothing \u2014 live, mid-sentence, in front of the customer.**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L56`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11850
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "checked": false,
          "line_num": 67,
          "tags": [],
          "text": "The fix for that is to use a few new ones from openrouter eg GLM and DeepSeek v4 flash. **Note:** DeepSeek v4 flash is the family that triggers [The thinking block eats the answer]; adding it repoints the demo instance into that bug's range, which the Monday-risk note currently rules out. Sequence the two."
        }
      ],
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "46fe21946d1ac384",
      "line_num": 66,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Four broken agents: four of five agents on try.sage.is answer `Model not found`. The connection serves one base model, `openai/gpt-oss-120b`; the other four point at `qwen/qwen3-32b` and `meta-llama/llama-4-scout-17b-16e-instruct`. `DEFAULT_MODEL_SELECTOR_FILTER` is `agents`, so the selector puts the broken four first. Decide: delete, repoint, or extend the connection. #interview ^c-agents**\n- [ ] The fix for that is to use a few new ones from openrouter eg GLM and DeepSeek v4 flash. **Note:** DeepSeek v4 flash is the family that triggers [The thinking block eats the answer]; adding it repoints the demo instance into that bug's range, which the Monday-risk note currently rules out. Sequence the two.\n\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L66`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 11940
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "b8be99932b4a58ef",
      "line_num": 69,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Tour route list: which surfaces are on screen Monday, in what order? This is the sorting key for every other card. It decides which surfaces must be fast, which must be clean, and which get judged on a phone. Name routes, not features. It absorbed the old cut line: 3.1.0 ships every server-rendered surface at once, so the only question left is which a visitor sees. #interview ^c-tour**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L69`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12030
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "8e963b6a55b73343",
      "line_num": 71,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Browser baseline: what does the demo path cost on try.sage.is, measured in a browser? `curl` reached 226.5 kB wire / 330.6 kB decoded across the shell's 11 assets \u2014 a floor, not a page cost, since SvelteKit pulls route chunks by dynamic import and none appear in the HTML. Every board number came from Cypress against a restored production snapshot (a different instance, 51 agents). Point `route-payload.cy.ts` at the live host. #research ^c-baseline**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L71`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12120
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "db17812ccc03e3d1",
      "line_num": 75,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Run-of-show \u2014 the three acts, timed: the sequence is decided; the minute budget and the two hinges are not. When do we stop listening and share the screen? When do we stop touring and talk terms? Who leads each act \u2014 Isabelle owns the narrative and the commercial reframe; Alexander owns the instance and the screen. Natural split, unconfirmed. The sorting key for the conversation cards, as [Tour route list] is for the surfaces. #interview ^c-runsheet**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L75`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12210
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "6de34a169e462475",
      "line_num": 77,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Kickoff agenda \u2014 which anchors survive: the listening pass assumes a cold room; R is not one. His pain, his seven systems, his objections, and his \"founding-partner not vendor\" ask are already in writing (`.clients.md`). Re-asking \"forget AI, what hurts?\" reads as not having read his email. Decide which of the five anchors get asked live, which compress to a one-line confirmation, and where \"which task comes off his plate first\" gets its first rung. #interview ^c-anchors**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L77`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12300
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "c68c755e6e765e6e",
      "line_num": 79,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#interview"
      ],
      "text": "**Objection \u2192 surface map: his email raised five objections \u2014 no proof of a finished product; price above his ceiling; wants a partnership, not a vendor; \"this can be built cheaply with Claude Code\"; an implied data-ownership and security expectation. Each needs a spoken answer and, where one exists, a live surface that proves it. \"Your data on your machine\" is undercut by the tour running on the shared, world-readable try.sage.is (tenancy note in Backlog) \u2014 handle it in words. Name which screen answers which objection, and which has none.**\n`#interview`\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L79`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12390
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "0ecb43716e13d7dd",
      "line_num": 82,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**The right-sized next step: the mirror-back ends by naming where this lands. R is Sage-shaped, so the bucket is not in question; the sub-question is the full seven-system build versus the CRM-and-ISA pilot he asked to start with. Decide the ask we want and the fallback we accept. #interview ^c-nextstep**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L82`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12480
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "4e3e9f9aedb5ef4e",
      "line_num": 84,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Mirror-back, verbatim: the pass works only if his exact words come back, not our paraphrase \u2014 paraphrase reads as \"you turned my words into sales language.\" His phrasing is on record (`.clients.md`). Pull the two or three phrases we mirror and draft the line. Prep-able before Monday. #task ^c-mirror**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L84`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12570
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "24f7e462fc358501",
      "line_num": 86,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Trial model set: which models does the demo run on, and who pays for the tokens? One base model is served today. Time-to-first-token: 1.62 s direct, 1.15 s through the one working agent, 16 SSE frames each \u2014 inside tolerance, on an unloaded instance. #interview ^c-models**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L86`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12660
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "c6ce717b5b95b134",
      "line_num": 88,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**School B's residue \u2014 REOPENED 2026-08-09: the tick recorded the DECISION to delete, not a purge. Root [TODO.md](../../TODO.md) still carries the purge unchecked, and [rows/school-b.md:11](../central-intelligence/rows/school-b.md) \u2014 written 2026-08-08, five days AFTER this tick \u2014 reads \"Their data is still on the shared demo instance.\" This is the only item on this chart that can end a sales call, so it is checked first. One authenticated look: knowledge bases, Space titles, prompt text, uploaded files. The two non-seeded residue agents can be deleted and stay deleted; the seeded ones come back at every reset. ^c-schoolb**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L88`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12750
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "4a266a6bd60eb7da",
      "line_num": 90,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**The `/api/models` cut: this card was found half-deleted on 2026-08-09 \u2014 the line ended mid-word at \"leave\", with no closing marks, no tag and no anchor. `charts/` is not in git, so the original text is gone. Rebuilt from the two places that survived it: the canvas node, which named the card \"The `/api/models` cut\" and asked for a `^c-apimodels` anchor, and the Screen inventory below. The question it was asking looks answered by its own evidence. On the board `/api/models` is 2,304 kB and fetched on every route, which is why it was ever a demo card. But [Screen inventory] measured it on this instance at 11.9 kB \u2014 the payload is large because of agents, avatars and knowledge, and the trial box has almost none. So it is not a demo problem. Close it as post-demo, and let it stand as one constraint on seeding: whatever [What replaces it on screen] adds, it adds to this number too. #interview ^c-apimodels**\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L90`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12840
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "527bc7235d4fd321",
      "line_num": 92,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#interview"
      ],
      "text": "**What replaces it on screen: the instance is near-empty, so this is the bigger half of the cleanup, not an afterthought. Five agents (four broken); five knowledge bases (three empty); zero prompts, zero chats, zero functions, one note. What does the tour show \u2014 seeded real-estate sample material, R's own documents, or an empty product we narrate? Nothing of R's can go on this host (tenancy note in Backlog).**\n`#interview`\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L92`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 12930
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/friday-demo/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "e1e9f1f27da9a6ba",
      "line_num": 94,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [
        "#task"
      ],
      "text": "**Phone-width pass: walk the tour surfaces on a real phone; record what breaks. The welcome page is no longer the one judged surface \u2014 on the live instance it is unfixed. The server-rendered replacement is on develop, so try.sage.is still serves the Svelte `TrySageWelcome` behind Reviewer T's bottom-of-page cutoff; the replacement is the only version that scrolls, and it arrives with 3.1.0. Phase S proved a green suite is the weakest evidence on an interactive surface, so a person walks these and a viewport assertion will not stand in.**\n`#task`\n`charts/friday-demo/TODO.md:L94`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 13020
    },
